WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.870
Zero Trust is a cybersecurity strategy based on continuously evaluating access rather

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.255
than assuming trust from network location.

00:00:07.255 --> 00:00:10.436
The familiar shorthand is never trust, always verify,

00:00:10.436 --> 00:00:14.013
but the deeper idea is to avoid implicit trust.

00:00:14.013 --> 00:00:18.783
Traditional security models often placed strong controls around a network perimeter and

00:00:18.783 --> 00:00:21.964
treated activity inside that boundary as more trustworthy.

00:00:21.964 --> 00:00:26.336
Modern environments make that assumption less reliable because users, devices, applications,

00:00:26.336 --> 00:00:28.721
and data operate across many locations.

00:00:28.721 --> 00:00:32.299
Zero Trust focuses protection on resources and access decisions.

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:37.069
Before allowing access, an organization evaluates who or what is requesting it,

00:00:37.069 --> 00:00:41.044
the device involved, the requested resource, current risk, applicable policy,

00:00:41.044 --> 00:00:42.635
and available security signals.

00:00:42.635 --> 00:00:45.815
Verification is not limited to the first sign-in.

00:00:45.815 --> 00:00:50.982
Conditions can change during a session: a device may fall out of compliance,

00:00:50.982 --> 00:00:55.753
an account may show unusual activity, a resource may become more sensitive,

00:00:55.753 --> 00:00:58.138
or threat information may raise concern.

00:00:58.138 --> 00:01:02.511
Zero Trust therefore combines identity, device posture, application controls, data protection,

00:01:02.511 --> 00:01:04.896
network visibility, policy enforcement, and monitoring.

00:01:04.896 --> 00:01:07.678
No single component represents the whole strategy.

00:01:07.678 --> 00:01:13.641
The goal is not to make work impossible or to treat every person as malicious.

00:01:13.641 --> 00:01:17.616
The goal is to make access explicit, appropriately limited, observable,

00:01:17.616 --> 00:01:21.194
and responsive to risk while still supporting business operations.

00:01:21.194 --> 00:01:25.169
Zero Trust also does not promise to eliminate cyber risk.

00:01:25.169 --> 00:01:28.747
It helps reduce unnecessary access, limit exposure, improve visibility,

00:01:28.747 --> 00:01:33.915
and make security decisions using stronger context than physical or network location alone.

00:01:33.915 --> 00:01:37.492
This course provides educational information about Zero Trust concepts.

00:01:37.492 --> 00:01:41.070
It is not architecture guidance, implementation advice, compliance guidance,

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:42.660
or vendor-specific recommendation material.
